      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "If $f(x)={4}^{7x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf={4}^{u},\\,u=7x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}={4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=7.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =7\\cdot {4}^{u}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4 \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot {4}^{u} \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡4\\cdot {4}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
